 - **set-beauvais-bridge.** A stone bridge over a brown river at dusk, a low parapet rail, a line of stones dragged across the road at the far end, picks leaning on them, the first star. No figures.
 - **set-amiens-inn.** Two views: an inn yard at evening with a well and lanterns being lit; a cellar door standing open at the top of stone steps going down into a dark full of barrels. No figures.
 - **set-calais-quay.** A stone quay in a grey harbour. Replace that foreground fence with one bare timber checkpoint gate crossing the landward walking route: set its long rail perpendicular to the quay edge, leave a clearly readable route from the town-side stones through the gate to the foot of the gangplank, and keep every timber face completely bare—no notice, paper, placard, inscription, or substitute symbol. Remove the foreground lantern, flame, and warm spill completely; suppress the warm window pinlights; relight the plate as diffuse grey morning; reduce the barrier gate's silhouette to secondary weight; and carry one uninterrupted, light-edged wet gangplank diagonally from the open stone quay to the boat so that the gangplank is the first high-contrast marker after the harbour silhouette and remains clear enough to stage the duel. A boat alongside; ropes, gulls, wind, spray, grey sea. No figures.
-- **set-buckingham-palace.** One unframed 1536 x 1024 landscape reference plate on warm laid paper, with four irregular vignettes feathered into open negative space and no straight seams, rules, frames, or rectangular divisions. Re-light every view with high-key, directionless white illumination and remove cast-shadow shapes; preserve depth through ink contour, marble joints, mirror edges, and restrained gold accents rather than dark tonal masses. Left: Buckingham's white palace front above a luminous river-grey river crowded with masts, with a broad marble stair descending unmistakably to the water. Upper right: a white-and-gold bedroom with a bed, tall mirror panels, and exactly one small white-and-gold bedside table; every other table and console is bare. On the bedside table, show one prominent open velvet casket angled toward us. Redesign this primary continuity marker as exactly two rows of six large, legible velvet slots, with exactly ten occupied by studs and exactly two conspicuously empty; remove every surplus stud and slot. The two empty slots must remain readable at reduced scale so later pages inherit the scripted ten-then-twelve mechanism rather than a generic jewel collection. No loose studs and no other portable bedroom objects. Lower right: a jeweler's workroom rendered in white, cream, and very light umber, with a pale bench, pale recess, pale bench fronts, and pale under-bench planes; no black mass or cast-shadow pool. On the bench, isolate exactly one pair of tongs and one compact circular loupe in a thin metal housing, shown side-three-quarter with the lens opening visible and recognizably scaled beside the tongs; the loupe has no brim, tall crown, vessel cavity, cup shape, or hat shape. Keep all secondary tools faint and subordinate. Exactly one small bench fire is the only flame and the only concentrated dark-warm accent, but it must not become a shadow-casting key light. Bottom foreground: a wide hall-of-mirrors study whose primary marker is a repeated rhythm of full-height mirrors in white-and-gold wall bays, clearly distinct from the bedroom. No figures, lettering, labels, or watermark.
